**Describe the bug**
The ```bt_id_create``` API fails to store the created identity in the Settings storage partitions under certain conditions.

The settings storage operation is scheduled within the ```bt_id_create``` API with the ```k_work_submit``` API, and then the Bluetooth identity counter (`bt_dev.id_count`) is incremented. The issue occurs when the ```bt_id_create``` API is called in the execution context, which has a lower priority than the work queue context. In this case, the scheduled storage operation interrupts the ```bt_id_create``` context and stores the Bluetooth identity item in Settings using the outdated value of the Bluetooth identity counter (`bt_dev.id_count`). As a result, the newly created Bluetooth identity is not stored by the Settings subsystem.

The regression was probably introduced in the following PR: https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/pull/51329/commits/ff9168b8ea6b592a5e7b60a51214b218fb69eb11 (https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/pull/51329/).
In the linked PR, incrementing order of the ```bt_dev.id_count``` variable was changed.

I think we should also consider simplifying the ```bt_id_create``` API and perform the Bluetooth Identity storage operation within the same context (without using the workqueue context). Other APIs, like ```bt_id_reset```, already perform the settings operation like bond removal (```bt_unpair```) without switching to the workqueue context.

**To Reproduce**
You can use the modified Zephyr Bluetooth Peripheral HR sample to reproduce this issue:

1. Extend the project configuration file `prj.conf` with the following entries:
```
CONFIG_BT_ID_MAX=2

CONFIG_BT_SETTINGS=y
CONFIG_FLASH=y
CONFIG_FLASH_PAGE_LAYOUT=y
CONFIG_FLASH_MAP=y
CONFIG_NVS=y
CONFIG_SETTINGS=y

# Increased stack due to settings API usage
CONFIG_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_STACK_SIZE=2048
```

2. Add additional code to the main.c to create a new Bluetooth identity:

```
static int app_bt_id_create(uint8_t id)
{
	int ret;
	bt_addr_le_t addrs[CONFIG_BT_ID_MAX];
	size_t count = ARRAY_SIZE(addrs);

	/* Check if the new app identity wasn't already created. */
	bt_id_get(addrs, &count);
	if (id < count) {
		return 0;
	}

	/* Create the new app identity. */
	do {
		ret = bt_id_create(NULL, NULL);
		if (ret < 0) {
			return ret;
		}
	} while (ret != id);

	return 0;
}

#include <zephyr/settings/settings.h>

static void bt_ready(void)
{
	int err;
	size_t bt_id_count;

	err = settings_load();
	if (err) {
		printk("Settings load failed (err: %d)\n", err);
		return;
	} else {
		printk("Settings loaded\n");
	}

	bt_id_get(NULL, &bt_id_count);
	printk("Number of BT identities after settings_load: %d\n", bt_id_count);

	err = app_bt_id_create(1);
	if (err) {
		printk("Advertising failed to start (err %d)\n", err);
		return;
	}

	bt_id_get(NULL, &bt_id_count);
	printk("Number of BT identities after ID creation: %d\n", bt_id_count);

       (...)
```
3. Build and flash the sample using the following commands (the nRF52840 target is used as an example):
```
west build -b nrf52840dk_nrf52840
nrfjprog -e
west flash
```
4. Observe the logs from the first sample run.
5. Reset the target and observe the logs from the subsequent sample runs.

You can use the following commit to setup the reproduction context: https://github.com/kapi-no/zephyr/commit/c0ee6e162f537fc4bb3f0fc89951e7c14ead7ff3

**Expected behavior**
The created Bluetooth identity should be correctly stored in the Settings regardless of the execution context in which the ```bt_id_create``` is called.

**Impact**
Configuration of the last Bluetooth identity is not preserved between system reboots.

